<h2 id="risk-and-benefits">Risks and benefits of using AI</h2>
<h3>Risks</h3>
<h4>Not being able to understand or explain outputs</h4>
<p>We often cannot verify what data the AI was trained on or how its algorithm works. We may not understand how it reached its conclusions or if this happened based on the data we fed it in our query.</p>
<p>AI outputs can be difficult to interpret. This can reduce the credibility of your work. For example, code generated by an AI tool may work, but contain vulnerabilities.</p>

<h4>Inaccurate outputs</h4>
<p>AI may provide false or misleading outputs (known as “hallucinations”), and it can be hard to assess their reliability. Gen AI models predict and generate content by determining the most likely subsequent pattern based on previous training. They are very good at generating plausible predictions that look right but may not actually be correct.</p>

<h4>Bias and inappropriate content</h4>
<p>AI reflects or amplifies biases present in training data. This means its outputs may be discriminatory, offensive, or not inclusive. AI may not always follow accessibility and inclusion best practices, even if trained or prompted to do so.</p>

<h4>Potential disconnect from reality</h4>
<p>If you are relying primarily on AI for your analysis, you may not develop deep familiarity with the data. This makes it harder to interpret findings accurately, articulate meaningful recommendations, and present them convincingly to stakeholders. As UCD professionals, fully understanding your underlying data is an essential part of our work.</p>

<h4>High financial cost and environmental impact</h4>
<p>Procuring, building, and maintaining AI tools can be expensive.</p>
<p>AI consumes significant energy and resources. When making the decision to use AI, consider whether you can justify this impact. Always use AI purposefully rather than casually.</p>
<p><code>Duplicates principle above?</code></p>

<h4>Not being secure or compliant</h4>
<p>Non-approved tools may process, store, or use data in ways that may cause security or legal issues. They may lack the necessary guardrails and increase the risk of data loss, exposing confidential or personal information. Do not put sensitive data into AI tools or use outputs from unknown sources. <code>Mention CoPilot?</code></p>
<p>AI models may be trained on unlicensed content generating copyright risks for the NHS. <code>There are probably other legal risks.</code></p>

<h4>Skills gaps and deterioration</h4>
<p>When you rely on AI tools without proper training, you risk poor outcomes, laziness or data breaches. Also you may lose your UCD skills through lack of practice.</p>
 
<h3>Benefits of using AI responsibly</h3>
<h4>Saving time</h4>
<p>AI can automate repetitive tasks, freeing you to focus on high-value activities such as sharing findings, influencing decisions, and advocating for recommendations with stakeholders.</p>

<h4>Reducing human error</h4>
<p>AI can help minimise manual mistakes by checking consistency in coding and flagging errors or things that are unexpected.</p>

<h4>Improving clarity and consistency in write-ups</h4>
<p>AI can help with proofreading, correcting grammatical errors, and making sure that we use a consistent tone and terminology across reports and presentations.</p>

<h4>Scaling analysis</h4>
<p>AI can process large volumes of qualitative or quantitative data quickly, making it easier to have a draft overview of potential outputs in complex projects or tight timelines.</p>

<h4>Generating new ideas</h4>
<p>AI can support brainstorming and critical thinking by suggesting alternative perspectives, highlighting patterns, and surfacing connections that might not be immediately obvious.</p>


